WEBVTT

00:01.700 --> 00:04.974
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:05.074 --> 00:08.360
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.394 --> 00:11.913
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.948 --> 00:14.915
♪ On which we used to rely?

00:14.950 --> 00:18.419
♪ Lucky there's a family guy

00:18.453 --> 00:21.738
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.756 --> 00:23.290
♪ All the things that make us

00:23.325 --> 00:24.675
♪ Laugh and cry

00:24.700 --> 00:25.200
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:34.219 --> 00:36.821
ANNOUNCER: We now return to
Germany's favorite talk show,

00:36.855 --> 00:37.972
<i>Das Ist Gut.</i>

00:38.006 --> 00:39.339
Dad, what is this show?

00:39.373 --> 00:41.908
I don't know. It's just
a German guy listing items

00:41.942 --> 00:43.676
and then declaring them good.

00:43.711 --> 00:46.362
<i>Wilkommen! Where are we?</i>

00:46.397 --> 00:48.815
<i>AUDIENCE:
Das! Ist! Gut!</i>

00:48.866 --> 00:51.634
<i>Okay, sunsets.
Das ist gut.</i>

00:51.669 --> 00:53.403
<i>AUDIENCE: Ja.
(applause)</i>

00:53.437 --> 00:55.071
<i>Sauerkraut.
Das ist gut.</i>

00:55.105 --> 00:57.140
(laughter, applause)

00:57.174 --> 00:59.642
<i>The autobahn. Vroom vroom!
Das ist gut.</i>

00:59.677 --> 01:00.977
(laughter)

01:01.011 --> 01:03.213
<i>Oompa music.
Das ist gut.</i>

01:03.247 --> 01:04.479
(laughter, applause)

01:04.514 --> 01:06.481
Euthanasia for
the mentally feeble.

01:06.516 --> 01:07.950
<i>Das ist gut.</i>

01:07.984 --> 01:09.618
(audience murmuring)

01:09.636 --> 01:12.037
<i>GERMAN ANNOUNCER: Das Ist Gut
is filmed before an audience</i>

01:12.071 --> 01:13.372
<i>of the mentally feeble.</i>

01:13.406 --> 01:15.307
(rapid gunfire, screaming)

01:15.341 --> 01:17.809
God, I wish I could get tickets
to that show.

01:17.844 --> 01:19.177
ANNOUNCER:
We interrupt this program

01:19.229 --> 01:21.463
to bring you a Channel Five News
special report.

01:21.481 --> 01:24.233
Good evening. I'm Joyce Kinney
with this special report.

01:24.267 --> 01:26.201
The latest police
statistics have revealed

01:26.236 --> 01:28.153
a significant rise in
crime throughout Quahog.

01:28.187 --> 01:29.454
That's right, Joyce.

01:29.488 --> 01:33.207
And just to put it out there,
Tom Tucker is packin'.

01:33.242 --> 01:36.277
I drive a 2006 Infiniti,
and I don't intend to lose it.

01:36.311 --> 01:37.979
So come and get some, punks.

01:38.013 --> 01:39.513
An increase
in crime?

01:39.548 --> 01:40.815
That can't
be right.

01:40.849 --> 01:42.650
I haven't noticed
anything like that.

01:42.684 --> 01:45.586
You're in the house 14 hours
a day. What would you notice?

01:45.621 --> 01:46.771
Well, I hate to
admit it, Lois,

01:46.805 --> 01:48.222
but over the last
couple of years,

01:48.257 --> 01:50.725
this town has changed a lot, a
not necessarily for the better.

01:50.759 --> 01:52.677
Yeah, I mean, the other day,
I left a doodie

01:52.711 --> 01:54.445
right in the middle
of a Crate and Barrel.

01:54.496 --> 01:56.496
Came back 45 minutes later,
it was gone.

01:56.530 --> 01:58.415
Yeah, even school's
getting more dangerous.

01:58.449 --> 02:00.233
The bullies
have been so busy,

02:00.268 --> 02:02.802
they're outsourcing
their bullying to India.

02:02.837 --> 02:04.137
MAN (Indian accent):
Okay, do you see

02:04.171 --> 02:05.572
the back of your underwear
in the mirror?

02:05.606 --> 02:06.873
Yes.

02:06.908 --> 02:08.909
Okay, I want you to pull it up
as high as you can.

02:08.943 --> 02:10.393
Well, I disagree
with you guys.

02:10.428 --> 02:12.178
I think Quahog
is still pretty great.

02:12.213 --> 02:13.580
You know, it's hard for me

02:13.614 --> 02:15.181
to take the things
you say seriously

02:15.216 --> 02:17.350
when I know what's been
in that mouth of yours.

02:17.385 --> 02:20.169
Look, I realize Quahog isn't
the small town it used to be,

02:20.203 --> 02:21.670
but it's still
very special to me.

02:21.705 --> 02:23.272
It's my home.

02:23.306 --> 02:26.108
Yeah, come on, guys, she's got
dyed roots in this community.

02:26.142 --> 02:27.643
Lois, I know you
love this town,

02:27.677 --> 02:29.695
but nothing is ever as
innocent as it seems.

02:29.729 --> 02:31.730
♪

02:34.200 --> 02:35.651
(grunts)

02:35.685 --> 02:37.369
Attaboy, Ope.

02:41.124 --> 02:42.441
(crickets chirping)

02:42.475 --> 02:44.576
Give me all
your money!

02:44.611 --> 02:46.194
Okay, okay!

02:46.228 --> 02:47.645
You have a white wallet?

02:47.680 --> 02:49.047
Yeah, just like
Truman Capote.

02:49.081 --> 02:50.415
Who's Truman Capote?

02:50.449 --> 02:51.849
What a surprise!

02:51.884 --> 02:54.819
The mugger's never
heard of Truman Capote.

02:54.853 --> 02:57.622
There's a library card in there!
Use it!

02:57.656 --> 03:00.224
(crowd chattering,
seals squeaking)

03:00.259 --> 03:02.626
And now, let's meet our star!

03:02.645 --> 03:06.764
Give a warm Quahog Oceanland
welcome to Bojangles!

03:06.799 --> 03:08.766
All the fish
in the bucket now!

03:08.801 --> 03:11.902
Not one fish at a time
as a reward! All of them!

03:11.936 --> 03:13.086
Okay, okay,
take it easy.

03:13.121 --> 03:14.287
Okay, the one thing

03:14.322 --> 03:17.157
you don't want to do
is tell me to take it easy.

03:17.191 --> 03:19.276
Now do that thing
where you rub my belly!

03:19.310 --> 03:20.827
I really like that!

03:20.862 --> 03:23.997
Oh, yeah. Yeah, yeah.

03:24.032 --> 03:27.868
<i>Yeah, now I'm gonna balance
a ball on your face.</i>

03:29.837 --> 03:30.971
One more push.

03:31.005 --> 03:32.139
(groans)

03:32.173 --> 03:33.607
There we go!
It's a boy!

03:33.641 --> 03:34.991
And he's got a gun!

03:35.025 --> 03:36.192
(two gunshots)

03:38.195 --> 03:40.880
(crying)

03:45.068 --> 03:46.519
Well, you guys,
we did it.

03:46.553 --> 03:49.155
We finally went to a restaurant
without somebody yelling at us,

03:49.189 --> 03:51.374
and then the rest of the place
applauding them.

03:53.410 --> 03:54.710
(all gasp)

03:54.745 --> 03:56.712
Oh, my God!

03:56.747 --> 03:58.080
We've been
burglarized!

03:58.115 --> 03:59.281
Well, we'll just have

03:59.316 --> 04:02.083
to get that $17 insurance check
and start over.

04:02.118 --> 04:04.185
Oh, my God,
it's so creepy to think

04:04.220 --> 04:05.653
there were strangers
in our house.

04:05.688 --> 04:07.155
I've never
felt so scared.

04:07.189 --> 04:08.490
I have.

04:10.860 --> 04:13.028
And now we wait.

04:14.196 --> 04:16.064
Oh, no, they took my gym shoes.

04:16.098 --> 04:18.767
Sorry, glutes,
no blasting today.

04:18.801 --> 04:21.469
They took my dirty rope
that was tied in a knot!

04:21.504 --> 04:23.738
They stole the chair
I use in the shower!

04:23.773 --> 04:25.889
They stole
all my pens!

04:25.924 --> 04:29.026
I... I don't have
a lot of stuff.

04:29.060 --> 04:30.094
Son of a bitch!

04:30.128 --> 04:32.463
They also stole
my sense of wonder.

04:32.497 --> 04:33.731
(birds chirping)

04:33.765 --> 04:34.865
Nope.

04:34.899 --> 04:36.934
Oh, this is horrible!

04:36.968 --> 04:38.235
Maybe you guys were right.

04:38.269 --> 04:40.637
Maybe our town has changed.

04:40.672 --> 04:41.872
I know, it's awful.

04:41.906 --> 04:43.607
Pretty soon, guys are
just gonna be showing

04:43.641 --> 04:46.076
their boners to babies
and stuff, I bet.

04:46.111 --> 04:49.480
It's sad to think that Quahog
isn't safe for families anymore.

04:49.514 --> 04:52.014
I mean, all I ever wanted was
to raise our children

04:52.049 --> 04:55.151
in a wholesome place,
far away from all the crime

04:55.185 --> 04:57.186
and problems of the big city.

04:57.221 --> 04:58.621
I'm reading you, Lois.

04:58.655 --> 05:01.090
I'm reading you loud and clear.

05:04.228 --> 05:05.661
(doorbell rings)

05:05.696 --> 05:07.063
Hi, I'm Peter Griffin.

05:07.097 --> 05:08.698
Um, I'm looking for
a wholesome place

05:08.732 --> 05:11.601
for my family to live, so
I'd like to buy your farm.

05:11.635 --> 05:13.035
All right, well,
the going rate

05:13.070 --> 05:16.104
for my farm is $875,000.

05:16.172 --> 05:18.106
Uh... nobody
called ahead?

05:18.141 --> 05:19.641
What?

05:19.675 --> 05:22.227
Well, somebody... somebody from
Fox was supposed to call ahead.

05:22.261 --> 05:23.528
They usually
take care of it,

05:23.563 --> 05:25.464
and then I just go
ahead and do stuff.

05:25.498 --> 05:26.932
I didn't get
no phone call.

05:26.966 --> 05:29.734
(phone ringing)

05:29.769 --> 05:30.802
Hello?

05:30.837 --> 05:32.037
Oh, yes.

05:32.071 --> 05:33.472
Yes, that'll be fine.

05:33.506 --> 05:34.639
Ah.

05:40.814 --> 05:42.515
Hey, Lois, you know
how I'm always

05:42.549 --> 05:43.983
buying things
impulsively?

05:44.017 --> 05:45.952
Well, that's gonna
be very hard to do

05:45.986 --> 05:47.553
now that we're
living on a farm.

05:47.588 --> 05:49.288
What? What are you
talking about?

05:49.323 --> 05:51.057
Um, a farm, Lois.

05:51.091 --> 05:52.525
It's where
vegetables live.

05:52.559 --> 05:54.093
Peter, you went out
and bought a farm

05:54.128 --> 05:55.862
without even talking
to me about it?

05:55.896 --> 05:58.164
Well, Lois, in my
defense, I have nothing

05:58.198 --> 05:59.949
to back up the beginning
of this sentence.

05:59.983 --> 06:01.217
I can't believe this.

06:01.251 --> 06:03.219
And it's not like
it's the first time you made

06:03.253 --> 06:05.771
a big family decision
without consulting me.

06:13.729 --> 06:16.798
(quietly): Yes, I'd like to
see your quietest doctor.

06:16.833 --> 06:18.500
Peter, where'd you
even get the money

06:18.534 --> 06:20.068
to make that kind
of purchase?

06:20.103 --> 06:21.069
I sold the house.

06:21.104 --> 06:22.471
And I did it
on Craigslist,

06:22.505 --> 06:24.206
so I also made a
dangerous friend.

06:24.240 --> 06:26.241
What? How could
you sell the house?

06:26.275 --> 06:27.843
Spooner Street is our home!

06:27.877 --> 06:30.178
I mean, I have three aprons
that say "Quahog."

06:30.213 --> 06:32.480
What do you want me to do,
start all over?

06:32.514 --> 06:34.194
Lois, you were saying
yourself how you want

06:34.216 --> 06:36.150
our kids to grow up
in a wholesome place

06:36.184 --> 06:37.351
away from the big city.

06:37.385 --> 06:39.587
I... I did say that.

06:39.621 --> 06:41.188
And, Chris, you been
telling me how you wanted

06:41.223 --> 06:42.423
to watch animals
humping other animals

06:42.457 --> 06:43.858
what might not
be okay with it.

06:43.892 --> 06:45.092
That's true.

06:45.127 --> 06:47.461
And, Meg, you yourself
said lip gloss unicorns

06:47.496 --> 06:49.597
Channing Tatum something
something bullcrap.

06:49.631 --> 06:50.998
You were listening
the whole time?

06:51.032 --> 06:52.299
Don't you see,
you guys?

06:52.334 --> 06:53.634
I did this for
the family.

06:53.668 --> 06:56.035
Just think of this farm as
our next great adventure.

06:56.070 --> 06:58.705
Like when we tried to
invade the city of Troy.

07:02.927 --> 07:05.261
Peter, I don't think
this is the right horse.

07:05.296 --> 07:08.398
I am 100% positive
this is the right horse.

07:08.432 --> 07:09.866
Come on, guys,
what do you say?

07:09.900 --> 07:11.434
Will you join me
in eating eggs

07:11.469 --> 07:12.836
fresh out of a
chicken's butt?

07:12.870 --> 07:15.538
Yay! Now I can be one
of those filthy teens

07:15.573 --> 07:18.174
with sad eyes
at a farmer's market!

07:18.209 --> 07:20.076
Well, with what's
become of Quahog,

07:20.110 --> 07:21.910
maybe it is time
to make a change.

07:21.945 --> 07:22.911
I'm in, Dad!

07:22.946 --> 07:23.912
Me, too!

07:23.947 --> 07:25.581
All right, let's do it.

07:25.615 --> 07:26.899
Hey, what's going on?

07:26.933 --> 07:29.535
Well, we've made a decision
as a family, Brian.

07:29.569 --> 07:33.472
We've decided to take you to a
nice, big farm upstate.

07:33.506 --> 07:35.174
Wh-What?

07:35.208 --> 07:37.309
A farm with big
open fields, Brian,

07:37.343 --> 07:39.645
where you can just run
and run and be free

07:39.679 --> 07:41.396
and no one will
ever hurt you.

07:41.431 --> 07:43.265
Doesn't that sound
wonderful, Brian?

07:43.299 --> 07:45.651
Are you ready to go
to the nice farm?

07:45.684 --> 07:46.717
No! No!

07:46.752 --> 07:48.119
You'll never take me alive!

07:48.153 --> 07:49.954
(grunts)

07:51.023 --> 07:54.625
Okay, I am at full
candy corn right now.

08:03.652 --> 08:05.386
(chickens clucking)

08:05.420 --> 08:07.104
Here we are, everyone.

08:07.139 --> 08:09.106
The Griffin family farm.

08:09.157 --> 08:11.708
Wow, look at us, Brian,
frontier people.

08:11.742 --> 08:13.810
I feel like those miners
who first struck gold

08:13.844 --> 08:15.212
in San Francisco.

08:16.247 --> 08:17.597
Oh, my God,
we did it!

08:17.632 --> 08:18.732
We struck gold!

08:18.766 --> 08:20.684
This is amazing!
We're gonna be rich!

08:20.718 --> 08:21.968
Rich and gay.

08:22.003 --> 08:23.119
Come here.

08:23.170 --> 08:24.571
(moaning)

08:24.605 --> 08:26.840
(cows mooing)

08:26.874 --> 08:28.675
Peter, what are you doing?

08:28.709 --> 08:30.460
Well, Lois, a big part
of owning a farm

08:30.494 --> 08:32.729
is crossing a road
very slowly with cows.

08:32.763 --> 08:35.265
Okay, okay, Bessie, now.

08:35.299 --> 08:37.166
Get yourself out there.
Easy does it.

08:37.200 --> 08:38.360
(tires screech, horn honking)

08:38.368 --> 08:41.537
Hey, come on, man!
I got to get to work!

08:41.571 --> 08:43.205
And so do they, sir.

08:43.240 --> 08:45.374
Hurry it up!

08:45.408 --> 08:47.192
Wait a second.

08:48.228 --> 08:50.095
Now get in line, you!

08:52.198 --> 08:54.867
Hey, I thought I told you kids
to go plow in the field.

08:54.901 --> 08:57.369
But, Dad, we've been
plowing all morning.

08:57.404 --> 08:59.138
Yeah, I can't take
any more plowing.

08:59.172 --> 09:00.306
I can barely walk.

09:00.340 --> 09:02.574
Look, I know it seems
like dirty work, but, Chris,

09:02.608 --> 09:04.709
you got to spread that seed
until your sack is empty.

09:04.743 --> 09:06.644
And, Meg, you got to clear
away all that brush

09:06.679 --> 09:09.013
so he can plant it deep
where it needs to be.

09:09.048 --> 09:11.065
Okay, we'll keep
at it, but I think

09:11.100 --> 09:12.767
that hoe is pretty
much worn out.

09:12.801 --> 09:15.019
Well, flip it over;
you can use both sides.

09:15.054 --> 09:16.504
(kazoo plays)

09:19.708 --> 09:21.843
Peter, there's a guy
standing out in the yard.

09:21.877 --> 09:23.144
Oh, yeah, that's Larry.

09:23.178 --> 09:25.580
Part of being a farmer is having
a best friend named Larry

09:25.614 --> 09:27.180
who I spend huge
amounts of time with,

09:27.215 --> 09:28.749
but never actually talk to.

09:28.783 --> 09:33.253
♪ Ooh, you make me
live now, honey ♪

09:33.288 --> 09:37.224
♪ Ooh, you make me live

09:37.258 --> 09:38.592
♪ Oh

09:38.626 --> 09:40.961
♪ You're the best friend

09:40.995 --> 09:43.597
♪ That I ever had

09:43.631 --> 09:46.533
♪ Been with you
such a long time ♪

09:46.567 --> 09:48.535
♪ You're my sunshine

09:48.569 --> 09:51.203
♪ And I want you to know

09:51.238 --> 09:53.205
♪ That my feelings are true

09:53.240 --> 09:56.258
♪ I really love you.

09:56.293 --> 09:58.711
Peter, I'm dying.

09:58.745 --> 10:01.080
This is the last time
I'll ever see you.

10:01.114 --> 10:02.248
Bye.

10:06.970 --> 10:09.088
Peter, I've been running
the numbers on this farm,

10:09.122 --> 10:10.756
and we're in deep trouble.

10:10.791 --> 10:13.092
We haven't grown
a single crop to sell.

10:13.126 --> 10:15.494
And meanwhile,
you're spending half our money

10:15.529 --> 10:17.228
down at the General Store,
and the other half

10:17.263 --> 10:18.830
at the Specific Store.

10:18.864 --> 10:20.832
Do you have almonds?
What kind of almonds?

10:20.866 --> 10:22.434
Marcona almonds.
From what region?

10:22.468 --> 10:23.435
The Basque Region.

10:23.469 --> 10:24.769
The Basque Region's
pretty big.

10:24.804 --> 10:26.004
North? South?

10:26.038 --> 10:27.005
Southern Basque.

10:27.039 --> 10:28.156
How much should
each almond weigh?

10:28.190 --> 10:29.190
Point eight ounces?

10:29.225 --> 10:30.759
Sorry, we only
have point sixes.

10:30.793 --> 10:31.793
Aw.

10:33.696 --> 10:35.347
PETER:
Yeah, hi. Do you have stuff?

10:35.381 --> 10:36.848
STOREKEEPER:
Yeah, we have stuff!

10:36.882 --> 10:39.384
And is it true that you've been
spending the farm's money

10:39.418 --> 10:42.686
on gingham shirts you tie into
a knot to expose your belly?

10:42.721 --> 10:44.188
I bought a couple.

10:44.222 --> 10:46.874
Well, with all this debt
and no income, there's no way

10:46.908 --> 10:49.209
we're gonna be able
to keep up our bank payments.

10:49.244 --> 10:51.011
Peter, if something
doesn't change,

10:51.046 --> 10:53.447
I'm afraid we're
gonna lose this farm.

10:53.481 --> 10:55.282
Great, just what I need
on my conscience:

10:55.316 --> 10:56.717
another failed business.

10:59.087 --> 11:01.422
<i>Hi, I'd like to rent
The Proposal.</i>

11:01.456 --> 11:03.590
Hang on, let me check
the return slot.

11:03.625 --> 11:05.192
I'm sorry, it hasn't
been returned yet.

11:05.226 --> 11:07.627
<i>I have a poster from The
Proposal you can look at.</i>

11:07.661 --> 11:08.911
You'll get
most of the story.

11:08.946 --> 11:10.913
Huh. They seem
pretty mismatched.

11:10.948 --> 11:12.181
There you go.

11:12.216 --> 11:13.366
Look, it's like
I've been saying,

11:13.400 --> 11:14.901
farming is
very difficult.

11:14.935 --> 11:16.669
When? W-When did
you say that?

11:16.704 --> 11:18.504
Farming takes incredible
skill and knowledge

11:18.539 --> 11:20.306
and the sort
of specialized experience

11:20.340 --> 11:22.058
that takes generations
to acquire.

11:22.092 --> 11:25.628
Dad, my favorite hen laid
this crazy-looking egg!

11:26.680 --> 11:28.147
Cheep-cheep.

11:28.182 --> 11:29.215
What's your point, Brian?

11:29.249 --> 11:31.334
My point is that
maybe I should go

11:31.368 --> 11:33.969
to the local state college and
take some agriculture courses.

11:34.003 --> 11:36.037
With the proper training,
maybe I can actually

11:36.072 --> 11:37.806
learn something that
will save this farm.

11:37.840 --> 11:39.274
Wow, Brian,
that's a great idea.

11:39.308 --> 11:41.810
And, hey, maybe I could give
the speech at your graduation.

11:41.844 --> 11:43.245
I'm really good at that.

11:43.279 --> 11:47.315
And so, as I look out at your
smiling faces filled with hope,

11:47.350 --> 11:50.252
all I can think is,
you are completely screwed.

11:50.286 --> 11:53.822
As Martin Luther King once said,
"Ouch, I've been shot."

11:53.856 --> 11:55.424
Thank you.

11:57.392 --> 11:59.026
Okay, that's
all my stuff.

11:59.060 --> 12:01.929
I'm off to State Tech Ag
Central College Tech.

12:01.963 --> 12:03.731
There's really two
"techs" in there?

12:03.765 --> 12:06.000
(sighs) The proudest day
of a man's life

12:06.034 --> 12:08.169
is when his dog
goes off to college.

12:08.203 --> 12:09.503
I'll learn all I can.

12:09.538 --> 12:11.622
And I'll see you guys soon.

12:14.476 --> 12:17.445
Aw, he packed a guitar?
What a douche.

12:19.214 --> 12:21.515
Guys, what's that over there?

12:28.773 --> 12:31.040
Oh, my God, it's a tornado!

12:31.075 --> 12:33.676
And it's coming this way!
We got to run!

12:33.711 --> 12:37.413
Hang on, guys, it might just be
the Tasmanian Devil.

12:37.448 --> 12:39.949
Come on!
Into the storm cellar!

12:43.788 --> 12:46.156
MEG: What is this place?
It's really dark down here.

12:46.190 --> 12:47.990
PETER: Shh.
MEG: What? Did you hear something?

12:47.991 --> 12:50.025
PETER: No, I just want you
to stop talking.

12:50.059 --> 12:52.227
CHRIS: Wait, I think
I found the lights.

12:53.997 --> 12:55.931
Wh-What is that?

12:55.965 --> 12:57.382
Aw, sweet!
It's some kind of lab.

12:57.417 --> 12:59.751
Hey, maybe it has
one of those things that goes...

12:59.786 --> 13:02.187
(imitates electricity humming)

13:02.222 --> 13:03.822
'Cause that's
important for science.

13:03.857 --> 13:05.424
Look at all this stuff.

13:05.458 --> 13:07.426
Burners, plastic tubing.

13:07.460 --> 13:09.428
12 cases of
cold medicine?

13:09.462 --> 13:10.762
You guys...

13:10.797 --> 13:12.964
I think this is
a meth lab.

13:12.998 --> 13:13.965
Oh, my God.

13:13.999 --> 13:15.600
You mean like
as in...

13:15.634 --> 13:17.468
D-R-U-G-S?

13:17.503 --> 13:20.304
Aren't those
I-L-L-E-G-A-L?

13:20.339 --> 13:21.305
What's going on?

13:21.340 --> 13:22.673
What are you guys
talking about?

13:22.708 --> 13:24.876
Uh-oh, someone's
getting cranky.

13:24.910 --> 13:26.894
I think he
needs an N-A-P.

13:26.929 --> 13:28.663
What's happening?
Where are you taking me?

13:28.697 --> 13:30.414
Man, I got to crack this code.

13:35.877 --> 13:37.159
Oh, my God!

13:37.194 --> 13:38.994
You guys, there's
a fully functional meth lab

13:39.029 --> 13:40.329
in our basement.

13:40.364 --> 13:41.931
Boy, we should
clean this place up.

13:41.965 --> 13:43.399
It's a meth.

13:43.433 --> 13:44.700
Brian?

13:44.735 --> 13:46.035
Oh, right, you're at college.

13:46.069 --> 13:47.303
We have to call
the police.

13:47.337 --> 13:48.437
Whoa, whoa,
hang on, Lois.

13:48.472 --> 13:49.705
Now, I know you're
freaked out,

13:49.740 --> 13:51.640
but when you really stop
and think about it,

13:51.675 --> 13:53.609
this meth lab is our
family's best chance

13:53.643 --> 13:55.111
to make meth in a lab.

13:55.145 --> 13:57.079
What... what are you
talking about, Peter?

13:57.114 --> 13:58.581
I'm talking about
saving the farm.

13:58.615 --> 13:59.949
Don't you see?

13:59.983 --> 14:02.384
This lab is just what we need
to get back on our feet.

14:02.418 --> 14:03.618
No way, Peter.

14:03.652 --> 14:04.953
Dealing drugs
is morally wrong,

14:04.987 --> 14:07.255
not to mention
incredibly dangerous.

14:07.289 --> 14:08.857
Come on, we've always
been risk takers.

14:08.891 --> 14:10.158
You remember
when we did it

14:10.192 --> 14:11.826
in the bathroom at
that restaurant?

14:11.861 --> 14:13.928
I can't believe
we're actually gonna do this.

14:13.963 --> 14:18.066
Yeah. You go in first,
and I'll go in a minute.

14:22.505 --> 14:23.938
PETER (moaning):
Oh, wow!

14:23.973 --> 14:25.507
Oh, my God!

14:25.541 --> 14:27.891
Oh, this is amazing!

14:30.929 --> 14:33.197
You dirty girl.

14:33.231 --> 14:35.666
Peter, I haven't
left yet.

14:35.700 --> 14:38.502
You sick whore.

14:38.536 --> 14:39.897
Look, it'd just be
temporary, Lois.

14:39.904 --> 14:42.773
Just for a bit, just till we
get the farm back on its feet.

14:42.807 --> 14:44.908
Till we get our family
back on its feet.

14:44.943 --> 14:46.376
I don't know...
Lois, we've always

14:46.411 --> 14:48.512
given our family
whatever it needs.

14:48.546 --> 14:50.614
Well, right now,
our family needs us

14:50.648 --> 14:53.166
to sell illegal drugs to
the tank top community.

14:53.200 --> 14:54.434
What do you say?

14:57.237 --> 15:00.373
All right, Peter, we can make
one meth, and that's it.

15:00.407 --> 15:03.376
And it's only because I don't
see any other way out of this.

15:03.410 --> 15:04.444
Thanks, Lois.

15:04.478 --> 15:06.078
Oh, this is gonna
work out great.

15:06.113 --> 15:08.281
Hey, are you guys
gonna feed us anytime soon?

15:08.315 --> 15:10.383
No, sorry, we're
doing meth stuff now.

15:10.417 --> 15:11.684
I'll eat that.

15:11.718 --> 15:13.920
You just got clean.

15:17.390 --> 15:19.291
Everyone, now that
I'm a meth dealer,

15:19.325 --> 15:21.877
I'm going to respond
violently to outside noises

15:21.911 --> 15:23.411
that the rest of
you don't hear.

15:23.446 --> 15:24.729
Uh, okay, Peter,
but I...

15:24.764 --> 15:26.681
What was that?!

15:26.716 --> 15:28.700
Dad, I think that
was just a cricket.

15:28.734 --> 15:29.701
Then what the hell was that?!

15:29.735 --> 15:31.069
That was just
me saying,

15:31.103 --> 15:32.404
"That was just
a cricket."

15:32.438 --> 15:33.405
Dad, settle down.

15:33.439 --> 15:35.307
There's no one
out there, Peter.

15:35.341 --> 15:36.758
You sure?

15:36.792 --> 15:38.426
Yeah, yeah, you're right,
it's probably just a--

15:38.460 --> 15:39.795
Did you guys lock the truck?!

15:39.829 --> 15:41.029
Dad, we don't
even have a...

15:41.063 --> 15:43.080
What the hell is
going on out there?!

15:43.114 --> 15:44.248
Come on out,
whoever you are!

15:44.282 --> 15:45.449
I want to show my kids

15:45.484 --> 15:47.084
what it looks like
when a bitch dies!

15:47.118 --> 15:48.252
Peter, there's
no one there.

15:48.286 --> 15:49.653
Well, maybe I
should just fire

15:49.688 --> 15:51.722
a couple of rounds
into the darkness.

15:53.725 --> 15:55.659
Don't hear those
crickets no more.

15:55.694 --> 15:57.495
I'm not...
gonna make it.

15:57.529 --> 15:59.163
You hang in there,
you hear me?

15:59.197 --> 16:00.364
Listen to me.

16:00.398 --> 16:02.399
I want you to take
care of my kids.

16:02.434 --> 16:03.801
I will.

16:03.835 --> 16:07.937
And I want you to be there
whenever there's a bad joke.

16:07.972 --> 16:11.174
Let them know.
Let them know.

16:12.276 --> 16:14.260
Gee, I hope I didn't
scare the cows.

16:14.295 --> 16:16.629
<i>I don't want to put
them in a bad mood.</i>

16:16.664 --> 16:18.481
(cricket chirping)

16:18.516 --> 16:21.818
(chirping, sobbing)

16:23.787 --> 16:25.705
(bird coos)
What's that, Dad?

16:25.739 --> 16:27.907
It's my new meth
distribution system.

16:27.942 --> 16:29.209
I've trained this
carrier pigeon

16:29.243 --> 16:30.860
to do all my deliveries for me.

16:30.895 --> 16:32.278
Fly away, Benjamin!

16:34.063 --> 16:35.497
Ah, perfect, perfect.

16:35.532 --> 16:37.666
Now this is the money
I owe Peter.

16:37.700 --> 16:39.985
I want you
to take it back to him.

16:40.019 --> 16:41.036
Do you understand?

16:43.373 --> 16:45.190
♪ Round and round

16:45.208 --> 16:47.142
♪ With love, we'll find...

16:49.412 --> 16:52.881
Yes, hello, I'd like to buy
400 blister packs of Actifed,

16:52.916 --> 16:55.183
or similar pseudoephedrine-based
decongestant.

16:55.218 --> 16:56.251
Why?

16:56.286 --> 16:58.920
I have a cold.

16:58.954 --> 17:00.288
Achoo.

17:00.322 --> 17:02.623
Hang on, let me
check something.

17:07.279 --> 17:08.613
Okay, you're fine.

17:08.647 --> 17:10.114
Be right back.

17:10.148 --> 17:11.215
Well done, Stewie.

17:11.250 --> 17:12.750
That could've gone really wrong.

17:12.784 --> 17:14.352
Like that Kid Rock concert.

17:14.386 --> 17:16.020
♪

17:16.054 --> 17:17.221
(gasps)

17:17.239 --> 17:18.606
Oh, my God, help!

17:18.640 --> 17:19.957
He's having
a heart attack!

17:19.992 --> 17:21.559
Is anyone here
a doctor?!

17:21.593 --> 17:24.427
No (bleep) way
someone's a doctor here.

17:28.332 --> 17:30.884
Hey, everyone, I'm back!

17:30.918 --> 17:32.252
Hello?

17:32.286 --> 17:33.853
What the hell?

17:36.407 --> 17:37.924
(growling)

17:39.560 --> 17:41.177
(muffled):
Hey, Brian's home!

17:41.212 --> 17:43.046
What the hell
is going on?

17:43.080 --> 17:44.381
Nothing, nothing.

17:44.415 --> 17:45.849
Just working on
a family project.

17:45.883 --> 17:47.117
Getting in some
together time.

17:47.151 --> 17:48.350
I have a crossbow!

17:48.385 --> 17:49.585
Chris has a crossbow.

17:49.619 --> 17:51.954
Okay, well, uh...

17:51.988 --> 17:53.722
I'm back
from agricultural school,

17:53.757 --> 17:56.425
and I might have the solution
to our farming trouble.

17:56.459 --> 17:58.410
I brought some
heirloom tomato seeds.

17:58.445 --> 17:59.812
That's nice, Brian.

17:59.846 --> 18:01.914
Here's a new kind of meth
you put in your pee-hole.

18:01.948 --> 18:02.948
Wh-What?

18:02.983 --> 18:04.049
Oh, yeah,
we're meth dealers now.

18:04.084 --> 18:05.050
Yeah, you missed it.

18:05.085 --> 18:07.019
My teeth came in
and then fell out again.

18:07.053 --> 18:08.854
I don't believe this!
Are you guys insane?!

18:08.888 --> 18:10.039
We're rich
is what we are!

18:10.073 --> 18:11.240
Look at all
that cash, Brian.

18:11.274 --> 18:12.707
(stammering):
He don't get none of that!

18:12.741 --> 18:14.075
He wasn't
here for it!

18:14.109 --> 18:15.143
Well, this is just great!

18:15.177 --> 18:16.561
I turn my back for a month,

18:16.595 --> 18:18.162
and this place
turns into a drug den!

18:18.197 --> 18:21.599
For your information,
it is also a drag queen hangout.

18:21.634 --> 18:24.235
Hey, look, our meth
is on the news!

18:24.269 --> 18:25.603
Good evening, I'm Tom Tucker.

18:25.638 --> 18:28.539
Our top story: Methamphetamine
is flooding into Quahog

18:28.574 --> 18:31.209
in epidemic amounts, and is
believed to be originating

18:31.243 --> 18:33.611
from the nearby
rural community of Farmton.

18:33.646 --> 18:35.313
Farmton-- that's the name
of the place we live.

18:35.347 --> 18:36.447
I forgot to mention
that earlier.

18:36.482 --> 18:38.281
For more on the story,
we now go live

18:38.316 --> 18:40.617
to Asian correspondent
Tricia Takanawa.

18:40.652 --> 18:41.952
Tom, meth is fine!
Everything's fine!

18:41.986 --> 18:43.303
Go back to your own business!

18:43.338 --> 18:44.504
Thanks, Tricia.

18:44.539 --> 18:46.673
She's doing fun things
for me, for money.

18:46.708 --> 18:48.025
Oh, my God.

18:48.059 --> 18:49.993
We're hurting
our own hometown.

18:50.028 --> 18:51.361
All those drugs that are

18:51.396 --> 18:53.513
flooding into Quahog
are from us.

18:53.548 --> 18:54.781
Oh, yeah?

18:54.816 --> 18:56.883
Well, if we're dealing
so many drugs to Quahog,

18:56.918 --> 18:59.102
why is the Quahog envelope
so light this week?

18:59.137 --> 19:00.821
Yeah, I say
we go down there

19:00.855 --> 19:02.806
and talk to
those people in Quahog.

19:02.856 --> 19:05.124
No! This is
what I'm talking about.

19:05.158 --> 19:07.259
Meth has turned
us into monsters.

19:07.294 --> 19:09.261
Lois, take it easy.
No. You know what?

19:09.296 --> 19:11.263
This is my own fault
for listening to you.

19:11.298 --> 19:13.332
I forgot you don't even
know your ass

19:13.367 --> 19:15.034
from a hole
in the ground.

19:16.053 --> 19:19.205
Is that... is that my ass?

19:19.239 --> 19:21.073
Lois, where
the hell you going?

19:21.107 --> 19:23.759
I'm going back home to
Quahog, where I belong.

19:23.794 --> 19:26.629
If any of you want to come
with me, you're welcome to.

19:26.663 --> 19:28.163
First of all,
you sat in something green

19:28.197 --> 19:30.298
and it's on your butt,
so no one's gonna follow you.

19:30.332 --> 19:32.200
And second,
no one's gonna leave a place

19:32.234 --> 19:33.435
where there are
tons of beakers

19:33.469 --> 19:35.370
for a place where
there are zero beakers.

19:35.404 --> 19:36.871
Peter, don't you see?

19:36.906 --> 19:39.340
We left Quahog because
of the crime problem.

19:39.375 --> 19:42.210
But now we've become
part of that problem.

19:42.244 --> 19:44.112
Yo, what is this bitch
trippin' about?!

19:44.146 --> 19:45.380
Look at our
lives, Peter.

19:45.414 --> 19:46.748
Look at our family.

19:46.782 --> 19:48.917
I don't recognize
us anymore.

19:48.951 --> 19:50.852
It's time to go back
to Spooner Street

19:50.886 --> 19:52.887
and be the
Griffins again.

19:54.956 --> 19:56.356
Let's go, Peter.

19:56.391 --> 19:58.392
Let's go back
to Quahog.

19:58.394 --> 20:00.427
But... but what about
this farm?

20:00.462 --> 20:01.729
And this house?

20:01.763 --> 20:04.431
And the lab inside the house,
which is currently producing

20:04.466 --> 20:06.266
an incredibly volatile
and explosive batch

20:06.301 --> 20:07.818
of thermo-reactive chemic...

20:15.059 --> 20:18.244
There were a lot of
Rottweilers in that house.

20:21.615 --> 20:23.983
Well, it's good to be
back in our old house.

20:24.017 --> 20:26.085
How did we get this place
back, by the way?

20:26.120 --> 20:28.121
Well, luckily the value
of this place plummeted

20:28.155 --> 20:29.589
because this is
a meth town now.

20:29.623 --> 20:31.657
You know, I think the
lesson here is that

20:31.692 --> 20:35.094
if your community has problems,
you don't abandon it.

20:35.129 --> 20:37.914
It's better to stay put and
help fix those problems.

20:37.948 --> 20:39.749
That's right, Lois, 'cause
wherever you run to,

20:39.783 --> 20:41.851
your problems have a
funny way of finding you.

20:41.885 --> 20:42.852
(knocking)

20:42.886 --> 20:44.653
WOMAN:
Hey, are you guys in there?

20:44.687 --> 20:46.521
Peter, I need a taste!

20:47.590 --> 20:48.830
Oh, my God, it's Jodie Sweetin.

20:48.841 --> 20:50.175
Quick, everyone
lie on the floor.

20:51.073 --> 20:54.265
JODIE: Peter, come on! I've got
Maureen McCormick with me.

20:54.266 --> 20:55.666
She'll let you
finish anywhere.

20:55.700 --> 20:58.815
Okay, everyone hide upstairs.
Daddy's gonna take care of this.

20:58.816 --> 21:59.816
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru